            <div xml:id="d693">

                <pb facs="0720" n="616" sameAs="frus:frus1932v05/pg616"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">816.00 Revolutions/63: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Whitehouse</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-01-23T22:00:00-07:00">January 23,
                            1932—10 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 24—2:39 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8. Have just seen the Minister for Foreign Affairs who guarantees that no
                    incursions into Salvador will be permitted from Guatemala. Everything normal
                    here but the frontier guards will be reenforced in view of the fears of the
                    Salvadorean authorities. I have notified the Legation at San Salvador of the
                    above.</p>
                <p>The Minister for Foreign Affairs however requested me to invite your attention to
                    two points which the Guatemalan Government considers of vital importance.</p>
                <list>
                    <item>First, that if the communist movement gets the upper hand in Salvador no
                        time should be lost in crushing it in order to prevent its spread to
                        Honduras and they are willing to take active steps to this end if and when
                        you authorize it:</item>
                    <item>Second, that pressure be used on Martínez to be sure that he lives up to the agreement to
                        turn over the Presidency to Gómez
                            Zárate when the Assembly has elected the latter First
                        Designate, as the Guatemalan Government seems to feel that Martínez might try to profit by the present
                        conditions.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Whitehouse</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
